# Events XML Event Schema

## Purpose

This is the informal XML schema for the single-prompt event pipeline. It describes a response format where the model reads one chat entry or prose segment and emits an `<events>` block. It is not an XSD document.

The XML element names use camelCase. Section headings use snake_case labels for readability; the XML examples show the exact tag names the model should emit.

## Top-Level Shape

The response is a flat chronological list of event elements. Player and party movement is handled by the prose response and must not be emitted during event extraction.

```xml
<events>
  <!-- Zero or more event elements in the order they happened in the text. -->
</events>
```

Rules:

- Omit event tags when that event did not occur.
- Repeat event tags for multiple instances.
- Do not emit player or party movement or arrival markers. The prose response owns travel detection and location changes.
{#
Player-movement event guidance is intentionally disabled. Keep it here so the
parser/executor contract remains documented beside the prompt if event-driven
movement is ever restored, but do not render it into event-check prompts.

{% if tinyBrainEventSchema %}
- Do not emit player or party movement or arrival markers. The prose has already been assigned to its correct movement phase.
{% else %}
- If no player/party travel occurred, all event elements remain direct children of `<events>` and belong to the active location.
- The first `<moveLocation>` or `<moveNewLocation>` element is the travel boundary. Events before it happened at the origin; events after `<arriveAtLocation/>` happened after arrival at the destination. As such, it is important that events be listed in chronological order.
- Omit any events that occur chronologically between the beginning and end of travel.
- Do not emit more than one player/party travel event in a single `<events>` block.
{% endif %}
#}
- `newExitDiscovered` is a normal event and does not create a context boundary.
- Emit one element for each observed event. Downstream processing may aggregate compatible entries later.
